      null, $t$Power is not the total energy: it is energy per second. Pump P: 6000 ÷ 20 = 300 W; pump Q: 9000 ÷ 45 = 200 W. So P has the greater power.$t$, $t$Both numbers are larger for Q, but not in the same proportion. Pump P: 6000 ÷ 20 = 300 W; pump Q: 9000 ÷ 45 = 200 W.$t$, $t$Running for longer does not raise the power. Power = energy ÷ time: pump P is 300 W and pump Q is 200 W.$t$, $t$Power = energy ÷ time. Pump P: 6000 ÷ 20 = 300 W. Pump Q: 9000 ÷ 45 = 200 W. Pump P transfers more energy each second, so it has the greater power, even though Q transfers more energy in total.$t$,
